            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Nobel's Business Ventures</strong><br>\nFollowing his invention of dynamite, Nobel established companies in more than 20 countries. He accumulated significant wealth from his international business endeavors. Nobel continued to invent new explosives and patents, which made him a leading figure in industrial chemistry.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel's Other Inventions</strong><br>\nBesides dynamite, Nobel held 355 different patents for his inventions. These included a more stable form of nitroglycerin called gelignite and Ballistite, a precursor to many modern smokeless powder explosives. His continuous innovations played a pivotal role in industrial developments of the late 19th century.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel's Literary Interest</strong><br>\nAlfred Nobel had a profound interest in literature and wrote several plays and poems during his lifetime. He was fluent in multiple languages and maintained a rich correspondence with prominent intellectuals and writers of his time. Literature was a comfort and a private passion for Nobel.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
